  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThis Afternoon and Tonight:\n\nSurface high pressure is pushing southward across the region \nthis afternoon, with winds turning to the south across most of\nnortheast MN, with more variable directions over northwest WI. \nMostly sunny skies have pushed temps into the upper 70s to low \n80s for most. Expect locations closer to Lake Superior to remain\nin low to mid 70s. \n\nTonight, onshore flow could lead to areas of fog at the head of\nthe Lake and into portions of the NW WI. Probabilities of vis\nbelow 0.5 mile are lower tonight, with less coverage than we saw\nthis morning. \n\nThe mid level ridge axis will cross the area tonight, with some\nmoisture increasing as low level winds become southerly. CAMs \nshow a scattering of showers overnight, with with a touch of \ndrier air in the low levels will maintain a dry forecast \novernight.\n\nMonday/Tuesday:\n\nHigh pressure is pushed to the southeast by tomorrow morning, \nallowing for broad southerly winds and warm air advection to \ntake hold of the region. During the morning hours a strong low \nlevel jet is expected to develop across the Northern Plains \nwhich will increase the chances of some elevated storms to \ndevelop in the Red River Valley. Expect to see storms to our\nwest (and possibly north) Monday morning. As that activity \npropagates northeastward, a storm or two could move into \nnorthwestern portions of the forecast area. A couple hundred \nJ/kg of MUCAPE, high lapse rates, and bulk shear of 40-50kts \ncould lead to severe storms that generate large hail. High res \nguidance continues to favor better chances for this scenario \nremaining to our west through the morning hours.\n\nA line of showers and storms will develop to our west ahead of a\ncold front Monday afternoon, and move through the area late\nMonday afternoon into Tuesday morning. Despite any morning \nshower/storm activity and cloud cover, the region will see ample\nrecovery with southerly flow promoting warm advection and \npushing CAPE upwards of 1000-1500 J/kg. Bulk shear will still be\nbountiful with an upper level jet rounding and upper level low \nmoving into Manitoba. Supercells will be possible capable of \nproducing large hail and damaging winds. Showers and storms \nwill continue to move across the region through the overnight \nperiod with the severe risk fading in the early morning hours. \nThereafter, showers and a few weaker storms will continue \nthrough the region Tuesday as the cold front pushes west to \neast. Rainfall totals from Monday afternoon through Tuesday\nmorning as high as 0.75in are possible across portions of Kooch\nand Itasca counties, with amounts dropping off to around 0.10in\ncloser to Lake Superior. \n\nMid week:\n\nHigh pressure over the region for Wednesday temperatures staying in \nthe 70s. Temperatures will slowly increase through the end of the \nwork week with intermittent rain chances possible as the upper level \npattern turns more zonal.|",
